        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "408c3968-323a-3296-81d9-1278565573ed",
                "word": "fiants",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "748d7add-1de7-3082-adfc-3b5a931e551e",
                        "entry_id": "408c3968-323a-3296-81d9-1278565573ed",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The dung of the wolf, fox or badger."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete",
                            "plural",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f6739571-0bd7-4554-a3d5-9ead14fddfcb",
                                "sense_id": "748d7add-1de7-3082-adfc-3b5a931e551e",
                                "text": "[If] they aske him how hee calleth the ordure of an Hart, Raindeare, Gote, or fallow Deare, he shall answere that they are to be called the fewmet or fewmishings[…]Of other vermine or stinking chases, as Foxes, Badgers and such like, they are called the feance[…]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b6e367d4-acd2-46aa-8831-2a09cef7e394",
                                "sense_id": "748d7add-1de7-3082-adfc-3b5a931e551e",
                                "text": "[Badgers] cast their fiants longer somewhat like a Fox[…]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e292bdbf-5c6f-42e2-8cc7-c6f40c269995",
                                "sense_id": "748d7add-1de7-3082-adfc-3b5a931e551e",
                                "text": "A man may know a dogge-wolfe from a bitch[…]the Bitche casteth hyr fiants commonly in the middest of an high way, whereas the dog casteth them on the one or other side of the path."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4ab2f456-b786-44ef-814e-d79f40d1d529",
                                "sense_id": "748d7add-1de7-3082-adfc-3b5a931e551e",
                                "text": "[…]a Deeres Fewmets, a Bore, or Beares Leasses, a Hare or Conneys Crottores, a Foxe or a Badgers Feance, and an Otters aintes[…]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1b4aad58-98e5-4617-a372-614d1c8b60e8",
                                "sense_id": "748d7add-1de7-3082-adfc-3b5a931e551e",
                                "text": "And these sort of Badgers where they have their Earths, use to cast their Fiants or Dung in a small hole, and cover it, whereas the Dog-Badgers make their Fiants at a good distance from their Burrows, which are deep, with variety of Chambers, Holes and Angles."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "53073fd3-7ec4-42fa-ab5a-dbe11403a5a1",
                                "sense_id": "748d7add-1de7-3082-adfc-3b5a931e551e",
                                "text": "He seldom travels above a mile or two from home, and generally to open pastures, and feeding grounds, in which the dung of the Cattle being turned over, is a sign the Badger has been there; or when heaps of moss appear collected in Cover, the fiants of the Animal being also observed."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
